From: Sobs <antohasobne@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wowbox64: the protection lock before the dynarec lock

A block being built holds the dynarec lock (mutex_dyndump) and protects
its x86 pages (protectDB: mutex_prot); a thread changing the protection
of memory holds mutex_prot and frees or marks blocks, which takes
mutex_dyndump (unprotectDB -> cleanDBFromAddressRange -> ...). In
wowbox64 the two met in Sid Meier's Pirates!: the game's thread and
WineD3D's command stream thread each waited for the other's lock for
good. There both locks are recursive critical sections: take mutex_prot
first wherever mutex_dyndump is taken, so the order is always the same.
Other builds are unchanged.

---
--- a/src/custommem.c
+++ b/src/custommem.c
@@ -587,6 +587,29 @@
 #ifdef _WIN32
 void enter_critical_section(void) {}
 void leave_critical_section(void) {}
+
+/* The protection lock first, see DYNDUMP_LOCK() */
+void dyndump_lock(void)
+{
+    mutex_lock(&mutex_prot);
+    mutex_lock(&my_context->mutex_dyndump);
+}
+
+int dyndump_trylock(void)
+{
+    mutex_lock(&mutex_prot);
+    if (mutex_trylock(&my_context->mutex_dyndump)) {
+        mutex_unlock(&mutex_prot);
+        return 1;
+    }
+    return 0;
+}
+
+void dyndump_unlock(void)
+{
+    mutex_unlock(&my_context->mutex_dyndump);
+    mutex_unlock(&mutex_prot);
+}
 #endif
 
 static void setProtection_generic(uintptr_t addr, size_t sz, uint32_t prot, mem_flag_t flags);
